				err = -ENAMETOOLONG;
				goto out;
			}
```
```c
 out:
	query_vma_teardown(&lock_ctx);
	mmput(mm);
```
Reason:
After the VMA locks have been released and `mmput(mm)` has already been called earlier in the function, the `goto out` jumps to the `out` label which calls `query_vma_teardown(&lock_ctx)` and `mmput(mm)` again. This causes a double `mmput()` on the same `mm_struct`, leading to a refcount underflow and potential use-after-free.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
			if (karg.build_id_size < build_id_sz) {
				err = -ENAMETOOLONG;
				goto out_file;
			}
```
```c
+out_file:
	if (vm_file)
		fput(vm_file);
	kfree(name_buf);
	return err;
```
Reason:
The `goto` target is changed to the new label `out_file`, which only performs cleanup of `vm_file` and `name_buf`. It does not call `query_vma_teardown()` or `mmput()` again, avoiding the double `mmput()`. This label is placed after the `out` label's teardown code, so that when execution reaches `out_file` from the error path, it skips the already-performed lock release and mmput.

